  "body": "<p>Kiralyn arrives angry and stranded, then finds people willing to offer a room, explanations and cooling water. With Dorothea she begins learning how her fire can close a wound, while ordinary kindness is still difficult for both of them.</p><h2 id=\"kiralyn-room-and-questions\">Finding a place to stay</h2><p>Kiralyn comes through the Gate bloodied, carrying a sword and struggling to understand where she has arrived. At the Observatory she introduces herself to Clive as a fire elementalist of the Ashari. Nébula has prepared a room, gives her a key and explains that the wider building stays open to visitors.</p><p>She is grateful for the invitation and the room. A new town feels like a maze compared with the paths she knew. The welcome gives her somewhere to ask questions, without establishing that she has already found a permanent home or recovered the people she lost.</p><h2 id=\"kiralyn-fire-and-grief\">Talking makes the fire harder to hold</h2><p>Heat builds in Kiralyn’s body. Dorothea finds her cooling herself in a tub and keeps freezing the water for her. The care brings them close enough to talk about lost sleep, pain and the people who hurt them. Kiralyn’s angry wish for revenge frightens Dorothea. Her attempt to apologize makes her own heat rise again.</p><p>Kiralyn tells her about returning from fishing to find her people turned against each other, and about burning her undead parents after finding them with her brother. It is her account of the catastrophe before the Gate. Speaking of it overwhelms her; she runs outside and erupts in golden fire.</p><p>Dorothea helps calm her and covers her with a coat. Their sword and amulet have reacted to each other’s light. Kiralyn says a black-robed figure opened her portal and told her the fire came from a source other than Pyrah. Neither the objects’ response nor their theories identifies the rescuers.</p><h2 id=\"kiralyn-healing-practice\">Trying to make it soothe</h2><p>Dorothea offers to teach what her mother taught her. She explains healing through exchange and demonstrates a transfer that feels like frost. Kiralyn does not claim to understand all of it. She asks permission to try and concentrates on helping rather than burning.</p><p>Her flame becomes pink and soothing. Fresh wounds under Dorothea’s bandages close, while Kiralyn’s own hands burn with the effort. She tries more until Dorothea asks her to stop. The older burns persist. The scene establishes this attempt and its cost, not a universal rule that all healing must injure the healer.</p><p>The lesson ends with a bargain over water and cooling. Later in the public square, Kiralyn cuts cloth from her own clothing to shield Dorothea from sunlight. Fire is one way she can help; remembering how hot the sun feels to someone else is another.</p><h2 id=\"kiralyn-writing-a-new-place\">Questions she keeps writing</h2><p>She asks Marius about clothing, work and the islands. He gives explanations and cautions her about motives that others might exploit. She shows him her writing and wonders whether he could help edit it. He returns it as something she should keep developing herself.</p><p>Her questions about materials and order do not mean she understands the town already. Her wish to heal Dorothea does not mean every hurt is repaired. The current scenes leave her with a room, companions and work she can continue, while the origin of her power and her account of home remain larger questions.</p>",
